{
  "research_questions": [
    "Does the COMPAS algorithm used in criminal justice settings display racial bias in predicting recidivism risk, such that Black defendants receive higher risk scores or are more likely to be classified as high-risk compared to white defendants with similar criminal histories and backgrounds?"
  ],
  "data_desc": {
    "dataset_description": "The COMPAS dataset was published by ProPublica in 2016 as part of its investigation into the fairness of the COMPAS (Correctional Offender Management Profiling for Alternative Sanctions) algorithm. It includes data from Broward County, Florida, collected between 2013 and 2014, covering defendants’ demographic characteristics, criminal histories, COMPAS risk assessment factors, and observed two-year recidivism outcomes. The dataset has been widely used in studies on algorithmic bias, fairness, and transparency in machine learning systems applied to criminal justice decisions.",
